mula_english:
“Monks, between Gayā and the Bodhi (tree), the Ājīvaka Upaka saw me on the road, having set out on the highway. Having seen me, he said this to me: ‘Your faculties are truly clear, friend, the color of your skin is perfectly pure and bright! For whom, friend, have you gone forth, to whom do you give allegiance? Who is your teacher, or whose doctrine do you approve of?’ When this was said, I, monks, addressed the Ājīvaka Upaka with these verses:

‘I am the conqueror of all, the knower of all, in all things undefiled,
I have renounced all, freed in the destruction of craving; having realized (this) myself, whom should I point to?

‘I have no teacher, no one is found to equal me;
In the world with its gods, there is no rival to me.

‘For I’m the *arhat* in the world, I’m the supreme teacher;
I alone am a *sammāsambuddha*, cooled and extinguished.

‘To set the Dhamma-wheel in motion, I am going to the town of Kāsī;
In a world that has become blind, I go to beat the drum of the Deathless.’

‘As you claim, friend, you are truly an infinite victor!’

‘Those like me are indeed victors who have attained the destruction of the *āsavas*;
I have conquered the negative *dharmas*, therefore, Upaka, I am a victor.’

“When this was said, monks, the Ājīvaka Upaka, saying, ‘May it be so, friend,’ shook his head, took a side-road, and left.
